  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$S=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}].$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(S) & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd column of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(S) & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6(10-0) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
